/*
We can transform a string by repeatedly deleting one of its first two characters.

Observation:
For any string s, after some deletions, the remaining string must be:
- a suffix s[k..]
- and among the first k deleted characters, at every step we could delete only one of the first two.
This means we cannot delete two consecutive characters without keeping one in between, except possibly the very first deleted character.
Equivalent characterization:
String t can be obtained from s iff t is a suffix of s starting at position k, and
the prefix length k satisfies k <= 1 + (length of t), more directly via DP below.

A cleaner way:
Let dp[i][j] mean whether suffix t[j..] can be obtained from suffix s[i..].
Transitions when matching current character:
- keep s[i] to match t[j]: if s[i] == t[j], then dp[i][j] |= dp[i+1][j+1]
- delete s[i]: allowed only if after deleting it, the next step starts at s[i+1],
  and we cannot delete s[i+1] immediately unless we had kept s[i]. This suggests a 2-state DP.

Even simpler characterization:
After deleting characters, the relative order of kept characters stays.
And because from the front we may delete one of first two, every kept character except possibly the first kept one
can skip at most one character before it.
Thus t can be obtained from s iff t is a subsequence of s, and if matched positions are p0 < p1 < ...,
then:
- p0 <= 1
- p_{k+1} - p_k <= 2
- after last kept character pk, all remaining characters must be deletable, so suffix length after pk is <= 1,
  i.e. pk >= |s|-2? Let's reason more carefully.

Actually, if we keep positions p0, p1, ..., pm-1, then:
before first kept char, we can delete at most one char => p0 <= 1
between consecutive kept chars, can delete at most one char => p_{r+1} - p_r <= 2
after last kept char, we can delete any remaining chars one by one while last kept char is at front,
so no restriction at the end.
Therefore t obtainable from s iff there exists an embedding of t into s satisfying:
p0 <= 1 and consecutive gaps <= 2.

This means the matching is forced greedily from left to right with only two possible current positions.
For pairwise checking among many strings, use rolling hashes and a trie-like DP over strings reversed? But constraints are absent.
We need a robust solution.

Key stronger observation:
Any obtainable string from s is exactly one of:
- s itself
- remove first char, then continue similarly on suffix
- remove second char, which means keep first char, then continue on suffix starting at 2
This yields recursive language:
Reach(s) = {s} U Reach(s.substring(1)) U { s.charAt(0) + x : x in Reach(s.substring(2)) }

For checking pair (a,b), we can DP by lengths:
Let can[i][j] = whether b[j..] obtainable from a[i..].
Then:
can[i][j] = can[i+1][j]  // delete a[i]
          OR (a[i]==b[j] AND can[i+2][j+1]) // keep a[i], maybe after optionally deleting a[i+1] later? 
Need care. This recurrence is actually correct from the recursive formula above on suffix a[i..]:
Reach(a[i..]) = Reach(a[i+1..]) U {a[i] + x, x in Reach(a[i+2..])}
So to match non-empty b[j..], either obtain it from a[i+1..], or keep a[i]==b[j] and obtain rest from a[i+2..].
Base: empty string not among inputs, but for DP:
can[i][m] = true for all i (delete everything possible?) Can we always delete all remaining chars? No, from a suffix of length >=2 you cannot delete all, because when one char remains at front and another second, you may delete front repeatedly? Example "ab": can get ""? delete a => "b", then delete b => "". yes. In fact empty is always obtainable. So true.
Also can[n][j< m]=false.

This DP is O(|a||b|) per pair. Without constraints, likely acceptable only for moderate sums.
We optimize with bitsets? In Java, BitSet can do transitions by character, but recurrence uses i+1 and i+2.

Let's implement pairwise DP with pruning:
- if lengths differ too much? Any obtainable string length <= |a|, and also minimum? can be 0. So only need shorter from longer.
- check only ordered longer->shorter and shorter->longer.

Assume total input moderate.

*/
public class Main {
    static boolean canObtain(String a, String b) {
        int n = a.length(), m = b.length();
        if (m > n) return false;

        boolean[] dpNext1 = new boolean[m + 1]; // for i+1
        boolean[] dpNext2 = new boolean[m + 1]; // for i+2

        // i = n: only empty b suffix possible
        dpNext1[m] = true;

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            boolean[] cur = new boolean[m + 1];
            cur[m] = true; // empty string always obtainable by deleting all remaining chars
            char ca = a.charAt(i);
            for (int j = m - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
                boolean ok = dpNext1[j]; // delete a[i]
                if (ca == b.charAt(j) && dpNext2[j + 1]) ok = true; // keep a[i]
                cur[j] = ok;
            }
            dpNext2 = dpNext1;
            dpNext1 = cur;
        }
        return dpNext1[0];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int N = fs.nextInt();
        String[] s = new String[N];
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) s[i] = fs.next();

        long ans = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < N; j++) {
                String a = s[i], b = s[j];
                boolean ok;
                if (a.length() >= b.length()) {
                    ok = canObtain(a, b) || canObtain(b, a);
                } else {
                    ok = canObtain(b, a) || canObtain(a, b);
                }
                if (ok) ans++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(ans);
    }

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        String next() throws IOException {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            while (c > ' ') {
                sb.append((char) c);
                c = read();
            }
            return sb.toString();
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return Integer.parseInt(next());
        }
    }
}

